# Molt copy check

This repo runs Molt on every commit. Molt checks UI text against the Birdeye style guide. It prints a table with the columns Priority, Where, String, Rule and Fix.

Molt sets the boundary. You report what Molt found. The designer decides what happens next.

## When a commit shows a Molt table

1. Show the designer the table in chat. Keep the columns: Priority, Where, String, Rule, Fix. Add one line saying whether the commit went through or was blocked.
2. Then stop. Ask the designer what they want to do. Do not edit any file and do not run another commit on your own.
3. Rewrite a string only when the designer asks you to.
   - Change only the flagged string, nothing else in the file.
   - Use Molt's Fix column. If it gives no replacement, suggest one and wait for a yes before you edit.
   - Tell the designer the old text and the new text.
   - Commit again only if the designer asked for that.
4. If the flag looks wrong, say so. Suggest asking the content designer. Do not dismiss a flag on your own.

## Never

- Never rewrite, reword or "clean up" copy unless the designer asked you to.
- Never run `git commit --no-verify` or `git push --no-verify`.
- Never edit `molt.config.json`, `molt-ignore.txt` or the git hook to get past Molt.
- Never get a commit through by leaving the flagged file or string out, unstaging it, or splitting the commit. Tell the designer instead.
- Never write copy that breaks the style guide just to make a commit pass.

## Pull requests

After the PR is open, tell the designer that Molt also runs on the PR. Its findings appear as annotations in the Files changed tab. If a check fails, show the findings and ask the designer what to do. Do not fix strings on your own.
